steps.body:
OWASP gives broken authorization three of its top five places, because it's the easiest bug to write and the easiest to find. Finding it takes no special tools, only a changed number in a URL or an extra field in a body. It also slips past tests that only ever sign in as the owner, which is most tests.

## Objects: whose booking is it?

**Object level authorization** asks, for each object a request touches, whether this caller may touch this one. Its failure is called **BOLA**, Broken Object Level Authorization, and in code it usually looks like this:

```ts
// src/modules/bookings/bookings.routes.ts (the bug)
router.get("/:bookingId", async (req, res) => {
  const { rows } = await pool.query("SELECT * FROM bookings WHERE id = $1", [
    req.params.bookingId,
  ]);
  if (rows.length === 0) throw new NotFoundError("No such booking.");
  res.json(rows[0]);
});
```

The SQL even uses a parameter, so it's safe from injection, and it's still wide open. Nothing is wrong with the login either: Ada is who she says she is. The handler just never asks whose booking it loaded, so any signed-in member can read any booking. Roombook's ids are numbers, so trying 1003, 1004, and on is a five-line loop.

The fix is the ownership check from the lesson on permissions, with the caller in the query itself. Here is its method, cut down to the owner's branch:

```ts sketch
// src/modules/bookings/bookings.repository.ts, inside createBookingRepository(db)
async findVisible(id: number, principal: Principal): Promise<Booking | undefined> {
  const { rows } = await db.query<BookingRow>(
    `SELECT ${COLUMNS} FROM bookings
     WHERE id = $1 AND user_id = $2`,
    [id, principal.userId],
  );
  return rows[0] ? toBooking(rows[0]) : undefined;
},
```

A stranger's booking is never even loaded, so nothing further down can leak it by mistake. The answer is 404, the same as for a booking that doesn't exist, since a 403 would confirm that 1003 is real. The full `findVisible` adds the branches for staff of the booking's building and for admins. The first example below shows Ada's two answers.

### Make the safe way the only way

A check that every handler has to remember is a check someone will forget. Three habits make it hard to forget:

- Give owned data a repository method that takes the principal, like `findVisible(id, principal)`, so a handler can't load a booking without saying who's asking.
- Make new routes deny by default: they need a signed-in caller unless they're public on purpose. Express has no rule of its own for this: a route with no middleware is open. Roombook registers its few public routes first, then mounts the sign-in check on the rest of the `/v1` router, so a route added later starts closed.
- Keep PostgreSQL's row-level security from the lesson on permissions underneath, as a safety net.

Random ids like UUIDs make guessing harder, but they aren't a fix. Ids leak in links, logs, and screenshots, and an id nobody can guess, with no check behind it, is still a missing check.

## Fields: which parts may they touch?

**Property level authorization** asks the same question about each field. OWASP calls its failure **BOPLA**, Broken Object Property Level Authorization, and it breaks in two directions:

- **Mass assignment** is copying a request body onto an object wholesale, so fields the client was never meant to set come along: `userId`, `status`, `version`, or a user's `role`. In Express, it's one line: `Object.assign(booking, req.body)`.
- **Excessive data exposure** is the reverse: a response that sends every column, `password_hash` included, and trusts the client to show only some. The `SELECT *` in the first snippet does exactly that.

The defenses are ones you've met, now with a security reason. An input schema per operation lists only the fields the caller may set and refuses the rest (the validation lesson), and an output view lists only the fields the caller may see (the lesson on request data). Here are two fields of the PATCH schema, and the view:

```ts
// src/modules/bookings/bookings.schemas.ts
import { z } from "zod";

export const UpdateBookingBody = z
  .strictObject({
    attendees: z.int().min(1).max(30),
    note: z.string().trim().max(500).nullable(),
  })
  .partial();

// src/modules/bookings/bookings.view.ts
import { toUtcString } from "../../http/time.ts";
import type { Booking } from "./bookings.model.ts";

export function toBookingView(b: Booking) {
  return {
    id: b.id,
    roomId: b.roomId,
    userId: b.userId,
    startsAt: toUtcString(b.startsAt),
    endsAt: toUtcString(b.endsAt),
    attendees: b.attendees,
    note: b.note,
    status: b.status,
    version: b.version,
    createdAt: toUtcString(b.createdAt),
  };
}
```

`z.strictObject` is what turns `userId` and `status` into a 422 instead of letting them ride along, as the second example shows. The handler passes on only the parsed result, never `req.body`, so nothing the schema didn't name can reach the repository. On the way out, every booking goes through `toBookingView`, so a column added to the table later stays inside until someone adds it here on purpose.

## Functions: may they call it at all?

**Function level authorization** is about whole operations: creating rooms is for staff, and deleting them is for admins. Its failure, **BFLA**, happens when a route has no role check: often an admin route added in a hurry, or a DELETE that sits next to a protected GET with nothing of its own. The fix is a role check declared with every route (the lesson on permissions):

```ts
// src/modules/rooms/rooms.routes.ts
router.post("/", requireRole("staff", "admin"), createRoom);
router.put("/:roomId", requireRole("staff", "admin"), replaceRoom);
router.patch("/:roomId", requireRole("staff", "admin"), patchRoom);
router.delete("/:roomId", requireRole("admin"), deleteRoom);
```

Each line carries its rule, so a review sees at a glance which routes are for whom, and a route with no `requireRole` stands out. A member who calls one of these gets 403. Unlike a booking, a route isn't a secret, since it's in the docs, so there's nothing for a 404 to hide.

## Tests that prove it

These bugs hide from tests that sign in as the owner, so write the attacker's tests too: sign in as Ada, ask for something that isn't hers, and expect a refusal. One for each endpoint that takes an id, and one for each role-protected route, turns this part into something CI checks on every change, with the `testApp` and `bearer` helpers from the testing lesson:

```ts
// test/api/authorization.test.ts
import request from "supertest";
import { describe, expect, it } from "vitest";
import { ada, bearer, testApp } from "../support/app.ts";

const otherMember = { id: 2, role: "member", buildingId: null } as const;
const booking = { roomId: 1, startsAt: "2026-10-05T14:00:00Z", endsAt: "2026-10-05T15:30:00Z", attendees: 3 };

describe("authorization", () => {
  it("hides another member's booking", async () => {
    const app = testApp();
    const theirs = await request(app)
      .post("/v1/bookings")
      .set("Authorization", await bearer(otherMember))
      .send(booking)
      .expect(201);

    const res = await request(app).get(theirs.headers.location).set("Authorization", await bearer(ada));
    expect(res.status).toBe(404);
  });

  it("refuses room creation to members", async () => {
    const res = await request(testApp())
      .post("/v1/rooms")
      .set("Authorization", await bearer(ada))
      .send({ name: "Noether", building: "main-library", capacity: 4 });
    expect(res.status).toBe(403);
  });
});
```

